/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-31774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055225 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\mid - \\frac{4z}{7} \\mid$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93131466-fa61-511d-92d7-124bf55dab82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055646 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055646 #edb/ref 1061 25121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6f034d09-bf96-5515-9938-b1ba199611c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055647 #edb/ref 1061 25122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e001fa58-8252-57e0-8441-22d3eb0c4673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055694 #edb/ref 1061 36686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "907ede99-0507-5ffb-9c09-ed38cd197061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055695 #edb/ref 1061 3973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35abb884-8f5f-5a93-a517-42d78b328130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055696 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055696 #edb/ref 1061 3975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055696 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ad335be-4991-593a-923a-cdcb127545d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055696 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055949 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the absolute value of a number represents the distance of that number from the origin.\n\nLet's take a look at the number line below.\n\n![](images/e2/e20db43f722fc4db42fc845ab20a7eef2c06437cc1d2246e0aed47dc01107aba.png)\n\nSince $-3$ and $3$ are at the same distance from the origin, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & = & 3\\end{vmatrix} = 3\n$$\n\nIn general, for any real number $a$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-a & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify expressions containing absolute value. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055949 #edb/ref 1065 1550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055949 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "015d8989-8f7a-58b4-9428-f28935b67b2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055949 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055996 #edb/ref 1064 "The absolute value operation is **distributive with respect to multiplication.** This means that the absolute value of a product equals the product of the absolute values.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\mid (-6) \\cdot 2 \\mid\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this expression in two separate ways:\n\n- The first is to find the absolute value of the product. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}(-6) \\cdot 2 & &= & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second is to find the product of the absolute values. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}(-6) \\cdot 2 & &= & -6 & \\cdot & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a \\cdot b & = & a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value operation is also distributive with respect to division. In general,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right| = \\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}a &}{\\mid b\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055996 #edb/ref 1065 6011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055996 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1585ba98-a0d1-54db-b624-714c8520cbd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055996 #edb/ref 1067 "The Distributive Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
